  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Epilepsy of unknown type, provoked breakthrough seizure (G40.909)</content>. A breakthrough generalized seizure provoked by two missed doses and a night without sleep, after about five months without seizures. Lamotrigine trough 5.9 ug/mL, close to his reference level of 6.2, so he is taking it reliably now. Discussed by message with Dr. Halvorsen today, who advises no dose change because the seizure had clear provoking factors. [S]<list><item>Continue lamotrigine 100 mg twice daily.</item><item>Seizure-free count restarts; no driving.</item><item>Call within a day after any seizure; the shame he felt is understood, and calling is part of the plan.</item><item>Neurology review in January as planned, or sooner after another seizure.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Gambling disorder, lapse (F63.0)</content>. A lapse, not a failure of treatment: it happened outside the reach of his self-exclusion, without therapy, and with a night of lost sleep that also triggered a seizure. The weeks after a large loss carry a higher suicide risk. Safety reassessed: no thoughts of suicide, no intent or plan; protective factors intact; safety plan updated. [S]<list><item>Resume weekly cognitive behavioral therapy this week, with a relapse-prevention review.</item><item>Return to weekly Gamblers Anonymous.</item><item>Debt counselor to add the new debt to the repayment plan.</item><item>Naltrexone discussed as an option with some evidence in gambling disorder; he prefers to intensify therapy first, and it will be reconsidered if urges or gambling continue.</item><item>988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline and the problem-gambling helpline confirmed in his phone; a friend will check in each evening this week.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Seizure frequency, seizure diary, and seizure description</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>One provoked generalized seizure about four weeks ago; none since</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Neurology review</td><td>In January, or sooner after a breakthrough seizure</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Dr. Halvorsen reviewed by message today: no dose change; January review kept</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Report of a breakthrough seizure to the clinic within a day</td><td>After any seizure</td><td>Deferred</td><td>Not reported until today, about four weeks later. The emergency department examination was normal and no further seizure followed, so no harm is evident. Corrective action: the seizure action plan now says to call or message within a day, and the clinic will check in by message during future trips</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Adherence and missed doses</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Two evening doses missed abroad; every dose taken since</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Lamotrigine trough level</td><td>Once at steady state as a reference, then with breakthrough seizures, suspected nonadherence, or interacting medicines</td><td>Completed today</td><td>5.9 ug/mL trough after the breakthrough seizure, close to his reference level of 6.2</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Antiseizure medicine adverse effects (dizziness, double vision, unsteadiness, sleep, mood)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Trigger review: sleep loss, alcohol, overwork, missed doses</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Sleep loss at the tables and missed doses; gambling and seizure triggers overlap</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Seizure safety and driving counseling</td><td>At diagnosis and every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Not driving; precautions reviewed again</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>SUDEP discussion</td><td>At diagnosis and when seizure control changes</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Revisited because control changed: missed doses and nights alone raise the risk; adherence and sleep are the main protections</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Rescue medicine for prolonged seizures or clusters</td><td>If seizures are prolonged or come in clusters</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Single brief seizure</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression screen (PHQ-9)</td><td>At intake, then every two to four months while symptoms are present</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PHQ-9 9, item 9 scored 0; up from 5 after the lapse</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Anxiety screen (GAD-7)</td><td>At intake and with any change in mood</td><td>Completed today</td><td>GAD-7 8</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide-risk review and safety plan, with the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline</td><td>Every visit (gambling disorder and antiseizure-medicine labeling)</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Reassessed after a large loss: no thoughts of suicide, intent, or plan; safety plan updated; friend checking in nightly this week</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Gambling since the last visit: days, money lost, and urges (G-SAS for the past week)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Gambled on four evenings abroad with a large loss; none for three weeks; G-SAS 22 for the past week, from strong urges and distress</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Cognitive behavioral therapy for gambling disorder</td><td>Weekly sessions for at least 8 to 12 weeks, then as needed</td><td>Deferred</td><td>No sessions for two months: the telehealth sessions planned at the last visit were never booked. Harm: the lapse occurred without support. Corrective action: weekly sessions resume this week, and therapy for any future trip is booked before departure</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Self-exclusion from casinos and online gambling</td><td>At the start of treatment; renew and extend after any lapse</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Renewed at home and extended to the major international online operators he uses</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Financial safeguards (bank gambling block, co-signer for large withdrawals, limited cash)</td><td>At the start of treatment, and after any lapse</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Bank block kept; his credit card is now held by the co-signing friend while he travels</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Peer support (Gamblers Anonymous)</td><td>Offered at the start of treatment and after any lapse</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Back to weekly Gamblers Anonymous</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Debt counseling</td><td>Again after any new debt</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Appointment to add the new debt to the plan</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Medicine for gambling disorder (opioid antagonist)</td><td>Considered if psychological treatment alone is not enough</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Discussed; he prefers to intensify therapy first; reconsider if gambling continues</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Reach the lamotrigine maintenance dose without rash by the two-month visit. Outcome by 2026-05-05: Maintenance dose of 100 mg twice daily reached after six weeks; no rash [S]</item><item>No seizures for six months after reaching the maintenance dose. Outcome by 2026-10-06: Not met: one generalized tonic-clonic seizure about five months after reaching the maintenance dose, after missed doses and a night without sleep; the six-month count restarts [S]</item><item>Casino and online self-exclusion and a bank gambling block in place by the four-week visit. Outcome by 2026-03-03: Self-exclusion from casinos in the state and from licensed online sites, and a gambling block on his bank cards, in place [S]</item><item>No gambling through the eight-month visit. Outcome by 2026-10-06: Not met: no gambling for about seven months, then a lapse while traveling abroad, outside the reach of his self-exclusion; treatment intensified [S]</item><item>Written seizure action plan, safety counseling, and a SUDEP discussion completed by the four-week visit. Outcome by 2026-03-03: Plan written and shared with two friends; SUDEP discussed with the neurologist [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Evidence-based guideline: Management of an unprovoked first seizure in adults: Report of the Guideline Development Subcommittee of the American Academy of Neurology and the American Epilepsy Society (2015); Practice guideline update summary: Efficacy and tolerability of the new antiepileptic drugs I: Treatment of new-onset epilepsy (2018); Practice guideline summary: Sudden unexpected death in epilepsy incidence rates and risk factors (2017); Psychological therapies for pathological and problem gambling (2012); Gambling disorder (2019); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Adult preventive visit in two weeks. Nurse telephone check in one week. Neurology review in January. Next scheduled visit 2026-10-20: adult preventive visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
